    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a> painted the ceiling between 1508 and 1512 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a>. He was a sculptor who first suggested that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a> should take the work, and he had been promised 3,000 ducats.</li><li>Julius II proposed twelve Apostles in the spandrels; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a> negotiated a grander scheme with nine scenes from Genesis, prophets, sibyls and ancestors of Christ, and the finished work has over 300 figures.</li><li>Candle smoke and varnish dimmed the colours over the centuries; a restoration from 1980 to 1994 revealed brighter colours and drew both praise and criticism.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>According to Vasari and Condivi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a> painted standing, not lying on his back as the biographer Paolo Giovio imagined, and the final narrative scene, God in the act of creation, was painted in a single day.</p></aside>
    </section>

